  <h1>The Last Signal</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">April 2026</div>
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 2 of 2</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/the-last-signal?page=1#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><span class="page-next disabled">Next &#8594;</span></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>She did not turn on the transmitter. She stood next to it, her hand on the switch, and she did not turn it on.</p>
<p>But she left the power connected. She left the equipment in place. She left the possibility open.</p>
<p>The signal came through on a Saturday. Eleanor was not at the station — she was at home, in her apartment, reading the letters again, trying to understand what she had missed, what she had not seen, what the letters were really telling her. She was listening to the radio, her grandfather's radio, the old tube radio that she had found in the station and brought home and restored, the radio that she had turned on without expecting anything, the radio that was tuned to the frequency that WKAE had used to broadcast.</p>
<p>The signal came through at 3 AM. This was what the recording showed, what she captured on her laptop when she realized what was happening, what she went back to the station to verify, what she played again and again in the weeks that followed until she understood it completely.</p>
<p>It was not a recording. She was certain of this, after analysis, after comparison, after every test she could think of to run. It was not a recording. It was a live transmission. It was a signal coming through on the frequency that WKAE had used, at a time when no one should have been broadcasting, at a time when the station was supposed to be dark.</p>
<p>The signal was her grandfather's voice.</p>
<p>This was what she could not explain, what she could not accept, what she could not stop hearing every time she played the recording. It was Harold's voice — not an imitation, not a recording of a recording, but his actual voice, the voice she remembered from childhood, from the visits to his house, from the way he had said her name when she was small and he was still alive and the world was still the world she thought it was.</p>
<p><em>Eleanor</em>, the voice said. <em>Eleanor, can you hear me?</em></p>
<p>She could hear him. She could hear him clearly. She could hear him the way she had always wanted to hear him, the way she had imagined hearing him when she was reading the letters, when she was trying to understand what he had done and why he had done it and why he had left it to her.</p>
<p>She could hear him.</p>
<p>And then, after a moment, another voice came through. A different voice. Not Harold's.</p>
<p><em>This is not a recording</em>, the voice said. <em>This is not a message from the past. This is a message from now. This is a message from someone who is listening, who has always been listening, who heard your grandfather's signal and is sending this one back.</em></p>
<p>Eleanor played the recording again. She played it a hundred times. She analyzed it and tested it and could not find any evidence that it was anything other than what it appeared to be: a live transmission, coming from somewhere she could not identify, using a frequency that should have been empty, carrying a message that should not have been possible.</p>
<p>She did not know who the sender was. She did not know how the signal was being transmitted, or why, or what it meant that someone was broadcasting on WKAE's frequency, using WKAE's station, speaking to her in a voice that was not her grandfather's but that claimed to be a response to her grandfather's signal.</p>
<p>She did not know what any of it meant.</p>
<p>But she knew one thing: the station was not dark anymore. The signal was out there. Someone was listening.</p>
<p>And they were waiting for her to answer.</p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
